{
  "name": "1964",
  "era": 1964,
  "note": "The civil-rights realignment as it happens. Home-state bonuses are 3-5, still high but past the 1932 peak. Cross-pressured voting is at its maximum and its most consequential: southern Democrats voting against their president's bill, and northern Republicans delivering the votes that passed it. Thurmond's card is a Republican for the first time. The district cards are the ground the coalitions are fighting over. More than half this pack runs against its own party's line somewhere, and that is the history: 1964 is the year of maximum intra-party division, with southern Democrats voting against their own president's bill and northern Republicans surviving the Goldwater collapse. Both are insulation from the national tide.",
